          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Vertex operator algebras (or “vertex algebras”, for short) are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras"} algebras
            with a product-operation parameterized by points in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
          %p
            Vertex operator algebras equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virasoro+algebra"} Virasoro algebra
            encode the local (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus-0
            behaviour) of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-dimensional+conformal+field+theories"} 2-dimensional conformal field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
            and of the
            = succeed "-parameterized" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} 3-point function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            with field insertions at the points 0,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+field+algebra"} full field algebra
          %p
            The traditional definition of
            %em vertex operator algebra
            (VOA) is long and tends to be somewhat unenlightening. But due to (
            %a{:href => "#Huang"}> Huang 91
            ) it is now known that vertex operator algebras have equivalently an
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphisms
            are conformal spheres with
            = succeed "-punctures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mi conf
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cob_{conf}^0
            of 2-dimensional genus-0 conformal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordisms
          %p
            As shown by theorems by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yi-Zhi+Huang"} Yi-Zhi Huang
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liang+Kong"} Liang Kong
            %em holomorphic representation
            of this category, or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra over an operad
            for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            i.e. an genus-0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mi conf
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V : 2Cob_{conf}^0 \to Vect
          %p
            such that its component
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
            is a holomorphic function on the moduli space of conformal punctured spheres.
          %h2#standard_definition Standard definition
          %p
            (under construction) A
            %strong vertex algebra
            consists of the following data:
          %ul
            %li
              nonnegatively graded complex vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \oplus_{n =0}^\infty V_n
            %li
              vacuum vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |0\rangle\in V_0
            %li
              a shift operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: V\to V
            %li
              operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo =
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi End
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi z
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = Y(-,z) : V\to End V [ [z,z^{-1}] ]
          %p This data satisfy three axioms:
          %p (vacuum)
          %p (translation axiom)
          %p (locality)
          %p
            In practice, the most important case is when the shift
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is related to a conformal vector “the energy momentum tensor”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            whose Fourier components are related to the generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_i
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virasoro+Lie+algebra"} Virasoro Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vir
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vir
            = succeed "." do
              %strong conformal vertex algebra
          %p
            There is a notion of a module over a vertex algebra. A conformal vertex algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is said to be
            %strong rational
            if every
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h2#simplified_definition Simplified definition
          %p
            The following definition appears with increasing level of details in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Borcherds1998"} Borcherds 1998
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kim2011"} Kim 2011
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Bu2021"} blog post by Chenjing Bu
          %p
            A
            %strong vertex algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with a family of operations (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge0
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^{\otimes n}\to V[[z_1,\ldots,z_n]][(z_i-z_j)^{-1}],\qquad a_1\otimes\cdots\otimes a_n\mapsto a_1(z_1)\cdots a_n(z_n)
          %p such that the following properties are satisfied:
          %ul
            %li
              Unit axiom:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(z)=a\cdot z^0+z\cdot(\cdots),
            %li
              Commutativity axiom:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1(z_1)\cdots a_n(z_n) = a_{\sigma(1)}(z_{\sigma(1)})\cdots a_{\sigma(n)}(z_{\sigma(n)}),\qquad \sigma\in \Sigma_n,
            %li
              Associativity axiom:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_{1,1}(z_{1,1})\cdots a_{1,n}(z_{1,n}))(y_1)\cdots (a_{m,1}(z_{m,1})\cdots a_{m,n}(z_{m,n}))(y_m)=a_{1,1}(z_{1,1}+y_1)\cdots a_{m,n}(z_{m,n}+y_m).
          %p
            As explained in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bu2024"} Bu
          %h2#Properties Properties
          %h3#CategoryOfVOAs Category of vertex operator algebras
          %p
            Vertex operator algebras naturally form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (see section 2.4 of (
            %a{:href => "#FrenkenHuangLepowsky"}> FrenkenHuangLepowsky
            ). This is naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            with respect to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of VOAs (section 2.5).
          %p
            This is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the category of algebras over the holomorphic punctured sphere operad (
                %a{:href => "#HuangCFT"}> Huang
                );
            %li
              %p
                the category of vertex operator coalgebras (
                %a{:href => "#Hubbard"}> Hubbard
                ).
          %h3#ModularTensorCategories Modular category of modules over a VOA
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            over a given vertex operator algebra is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Huang"} Huang
          %h3#goddardthorn_theorem Goddard-Thorn theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goddard-Thorn+theorem"} Goddard-Thorn theorem
          %h3#RelationToAInfinityAlgebras
            Relation to
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from the category of
            = succeed "-VOAs" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+algebra"} A-infinity algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Zeitlin"}> Anton Zeitlin
              ,
              %em Quasiclassical Lian-Zuckerman Homotopy Algebras, Courant Algebroids and Gauge Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0910.3652"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            and argued to algebraically encode the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            background encoded by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            given by the VOA.
          %h3#relation_to_conformal_nets Relation to conformal nets
          %p
            Subject to some conditions, from a vertex operator algebra one may induce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
            and conversely (
            %a{:href => "#CarpiKawahigahshiLongoWeiner15"}> Carpi-Kawahigahshi-Longo-Weiner 15
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Carpi16"} Carpi 16
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Gui18"} Gui 18
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            A class of examples are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+algebras"} current algebras
            \.
          %p
            A database of examples is given by (
            %a{:href => "#GannonHoehn"}> Gannon-Höhn
            ).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moonshine"} Moonshine
            module over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Griess+algebra"} Griess algebra
            admits the structure of a vertex operator algebra, which has
          %ul
            %li
              %p rank 24;
            %li
              %p
                is a self-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dual object
                in the category of VOAs;
            %li
              %p has trivial degree-1 subspaces.
          %p
            A conjecture due to
            %a{:href => "#FLM"} Frenkel–Lepowsky–Meurman
            says that the Moonshine VOA is, up to isomorphism the unique VOA with these properties. See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monster+vertex+algebra"} monster vertex algebra
